    "scoring_3",
    "This exploration produces ONE mechanism. You are one of three independent reviewers; you see the same frozen evidence bundle and the same proposals as the others, and you do not coordinate with them before handing in.\nTHE RESEARCH OBJECTIVE:\nDiscover new mechanisms in which TRAINING-ONLY user behaviour and HYPERBOLIC geometry are computationally coupled to improve the RQ-VAE Semantic IDs used by a generative next-item recommender, with the real target of Stage3 test Recall@10 above 0.065.\nTHE SCOPE CONTRACT (a proposal that misses it is sent back, not scored):\nA proposal is IN SCOPE only when all of this holds:\n  1. BEHAVIOUR: it names one training-only behaviour signal (directed item transitions, user-interest branching, sequential co-consumption, popularity-adjusted graph neighbours, behaviour-conditioned assignment weights, ...) and says how it is computed.\n  2. HYPERBOLIC GEOMETRY: it names an explicit curved operation (trainable or fixed negative curvature, Poincare or Lorentz distance, exp/log maps, geodesic scoring, curved residual, curved quantization, curved regularization) and where in training or quantization it acts.\n  3. REAL COUPLING: one connected computational pathway uses both, so that removing either changes the core computation. A behaviour suffix on an unchanged Euclidean quantizer, a standalone hyperbolic loss beside an unrelated behaviour loss, a geometry name without geometric computation, or inherited unused hyperbolic code do NOT qualify.\n  4. TESTABLE EFFECT: it states what should observably change (SID assignment, gradient or parameter update, prefix behaviour predictability, or Stage3 Recall) and names the behaviour-off, geometry-off/Euclidean and both-on comparisons. The three switches must be part of the mechanism, so that the coupling can be shown to be real, but the round RUNS no ablation arm: each candidate is trained once.\n  5. MINIMAL SPECIFICATION: equations or pseudocode, inputs and outputs, numerical safeguards and the training cost. No proof and no paper section are required.\nSID quality (ER, prefix counts, collisions) is NOT a scope criterion and NOT an admission rule.\n\nEXPERIMENT POLICY (binding on every proposal, implementation and review):\n  - ONE candidate, ONE training run, on the frozen budget (3000 Stage 2 epochs on four cards, the frozen Stage 3 protocol, no early stopping).\n  - NO ablation experiments: the behaviour-off, geometry-off and both-on switches must EXIST in the code and must be correct, but no arm is trained with them switched off.\n  - NO tuning experiments: no sweep over the coupling weight, the clip bound, the temperature, the usage weight or any other constant. A constant that enters the mechanism has to be DERIVED (a scale-relative bound, a measured quantity, a documented default), not chosen by trying values.\n  - NO multi-seed experiments: a candidate is not repeated with other seeds, so a single run decides it and the seed noise is part of the uncertainty.\n  - The behavioural diagnostics (coherence, adjacency, transfer predictability) are recorded, never used as an admission gate; Stage 3 test_recall@10 is the judgement.\n  - A proposal that needs an ablation, a sweep or a seed repetition to make its case is out of programme: it has to make the case with one run.\nTHE SELECTION POLICY (rank-2026-10-v1): the reviewers RANK the proposals. The proposal the reviewers place best, in total, is the round's mechanism. Rank every proposal, best first, and give each one a reason that names the evidence you used.\nRead the frozen evidence bundle for each proposal before you place it: a placement you cannot ground in the evidence is not a placement.\nWrite PLAIN TEXT, one line per proposal, in exactly this shape:\nRANK: <idea_id> | position=<1 for the best, then 2, 3, ...> | reason=<one line>\nRANK: <idea_id> | position=<n> | reason=<one line>\nEvery proposal must appear exactly once, the positions must be 1..N with no ties and no gaps, and the reason must say what in the evidence put the proposal there. If you cannot ground a proposal in the evidence, rank it last and say so in its reason; do not leave a proposal out.\nTHE PROPOSALS:\nIDEA: CURVCAP-SUCCPLAN\nTITLE: Capacity-frozen, successor-curvature-field Poincaré assignment for the RQ-VAE, with the code radius left untouched by construction\nMOTIVATION: The prior round proved that the curvature signal tracks SID quality (Spearman -0.20) but spent that signal on the per-level code radius rho_l, collapsing the codebook: exported support 2/13/56, collision rate 0.998, L1 positive/negative hit rates 0.982/0.980, Stage3 test_recall@10 0.041888 against 0.055537. Curvature and resolution were competing for the same scalar. The fix is to change the currency: curvature may decide WHICH item occupies WHICH code, never how much of the ball a code occupies.\nNOVELTY: Unlike CURVALLOC (curvature -> c_l and rho_l), BRad-RQ (rank/radial assignment by context entropy), CURVCTRL-SID (prefix score -> per-level temperature), poincare-frechet (curved codebook update), and unlike a behaviour-weighted Sinkhorn with an additive Euclidean cost, behaviour here is not a weight and not a suffix: it IS the local curvature of the distance, c_ik = c_l * exp(-a_ik). The transport column marginals are frozen to the Euclidean nearest-code counts, so curvature is structurally incapable of changing code usage; that is the property the previous mechanism lacked.\nCHANGE: Replaces the per-level nearest-code step by a curvature-field, capacity-frozen optimal-transport assignment in which each item-code pair carries its own Poincaré sectional curvature, fixed by the fraction of the item's training successors already sitting in that code's community.\nSPECIFICATION: {'text': 'Per level l, map the residual and the codes into a Poincaré ball with per-level curvature c_l (from the standardised behaviour-graph curvature profile), price each pair by C_ik = d_{c_ik}(psi(z_i), psi(v_k)) with c_ik = c_l*exp(-a_ik), solve a Sinkhorn plan whose row sums are 1 and whose column sums are exactly the Euclidean nearest-code counts u_k of the same batch, take the code as argmax_k P_ik with a straight-through gradient through the soft code, and recurse on the residual. Column capacities equal to the Euclidean counts is the resolution guarantee: the per-level usage multiset, and therefore the exported support, is exactly the Euclidean one.'}\nCOST: Added ~108M MAC per step at the frozen scale (B=1023, K=256, d=64, J=10: 3*B*K*d for the curved cost matrix, 3*B*K*d for the Euclidean capacity pass, 3*B*K*J for the ten Sinkhorn iterations), about 0.22 GFLOP per step, i.e. roughly 1.0-1.8x the ~120M MAC RQ-VAE step. Over 72,000 steps that is ~1.6e13 added FLOPs, about 10-40 min extra on four cards including launch overhead. The curvature schedule (60 recomputations x 3 levels x <=256-node exact ORC) costs under one minute. The 256-per-level codebooks are consistent with the frozen 247/7414/20223 support; if one level used 4096 codes its per-step cost would grow 16x.\nHYPOTHESIS: If an item's level-l prefix should coincide with the level-l prefix its training successors already occupy, then letting curvature pull the assignment toward successor codes while capacity forbids any resolution loss should raise prefix sharing along transitions and lift Stage3 test_recall@10 above 0.065 (from 0.055537), with the exported SID support unchanged and with 5-40% of level-1 codes permuted relative to the geometry-off assignment. It is worth keeping only if both the permutation fraction is non-trivial and recall moves.\nRISKS: ['The curvature carrier is only a 3-vector, so bandwidth is low; a_ik computed from Euclidean successor codes may be noisy early and behave like a random permutation; the Sinkhorn temperature derived from the measured cost gap can be unstable in early epochs; capacity matching limits codebook adaptation; cross-level SID collisions may rise even though per-level support is preserved; the curvature may act as a mild regulariser with less than 1% assignment change and no recall movement.']\nEND IDEA\n\nIDEA: CURVCHART-EQUIMARGIN-RQ\nTITLE: Per-code chart curvature on the training behaviour quotient graph, spent as a hard-marginal entropic transport assignment that cannot give up codebook resolution.\nMOTIVATION: The previous round proved that the standardised behaviour-graph curvature profile carries real signal (Spearman -0.20 over 115 mechanism families on the level-1 minus level-3 deviation) but that spending it on the per-level code radius destroys the thing recall actually needs: resolution. That run exported 2/13/56 SIDs, collision 0.998, level-1 positive/negative hit rates 0.982/0.980 (the first token carried no information), and scored 0.041888. The underlying pathology is level collapse in the first RQ level: 2 of 256 codes alive. This idea gives the curvature a place to act where it cannot shrink anything, and separately removes the collapse by construction.\nNOVELTY: Three things absent from CURVALLOC-LORENTZ-RQ, BRad-RQ, CURVCTRL-SID, behcone-prefix-rq, poincare-frechet-successor-codebook, succboost-geoshift, MIXCURV-FIBRE-RQ, LORENTZ-SUCCPROFILE-SMOOTH-RQ and BRANCHCURV-MOEB-RQ. (1) Curvature is a FIELD, one value per code per level, `kappa_{l,k} = kappa_0 * exp(alpha * kappa_hat_{l,k})`; all earlier mechanisms set one number per level and applied it to the whole level. (2) Curvature acts on the DISTANCE CHART of each code, never on the code vector, its norm, its count or the space's radius; the code table keeps the inherited parameterisation bit for bit. (3) The assignment is an entropic optimal transport problem with hard uniform column marginals, so code utilisation is fixed at B/K by the constraint, not by a loss weight; the resolution guarantee is a constraint, not a hyperparameter. Curvature reorders who goes where and is structurally forbidden from changing how many codes are used.\nCHANGE: Each RQ level solves its assignment as a Sinkhorn transport problem whose per-code cost uses a Poincare distance in the code's own curvature chart `kappa_{l,k}`, with uniform column marginals; the per-code curvatures come from the standardised Ollivier curvature of the K-node code-transition quotient graph built from training transitions under the previous sync's memberships, so curvature can only permute items between codes, never remove codes.\nSPECIFICATION: {'text': \"Notation: level l in {1,2,3}, K_l codes per level, batch B items (B = 1023 at the frozen scale).\\n  Code table: `c_{l,k} in R^d`, inherited parameterisation, inherited init, inherited K_l. Untouched by this mechanism.\\n  Residual: `x_i^(1) = E(u_i)`; `x_i^(l+1) = x_i^(l) - c_{l, a_i^(l)}`.\\n  Scale: `s_l = 0.99*s_l + 0.01*RMS(||x_i^(l)||)`. Curvature base `kappa_0 = 1/(s_l^2 + 1e-8)` (derived from the measured residual scale, no free constant).\\n  Ball embedding: `rho_k(z) = (tanh(sqrt(k)||z||/2) / (sqrt(k)||z||)) * z`, `rho_k(0)=0`.\\n  Poincare distance: `d_k(x,y) = (2/sqrt(k)) * artanh( sqrt(k) * || (-x)(+)y || )`, with `(-x)(+)y = ((1-k|x|^2)y - (1-k|y|^2)x) / (1 - 2k<x,y> + k^2|x|^2|y|^2)`.\\n  Local chart distance (the object the mechanism spends): `dhat_k(x,y) = d_k(x,y) / lambda_k(y)`, `lambda_k(y) = 2/(1 - k|y|^2)`. Note `dhat_k -> ||x-y||` as `k -> 0`.\\n  Assignment cost: `C_l[i,k] = dhat_{kappa_{l,k}}( rho_{kappa_{l,k}}(x_i^(l)), rho_{kappa_{l,k}}(c_{l,k}) )^2 / eps_l`, `eps_l = median(C_l)` detached.\\n  Transport: `P_l = Sinkhorn(C_l, r = 1/B, c = 1/K_l, iters = 8)` on kernel `exp(-C_l)`.\\n  Forward assignment `a_i^(l) = argmax_k P_l[i,k]`; straight-through: code term uses `sum_k P_l[i,k] c_{l,k}` with argmax as the forward value and the soft plan as the backward gradient.\\n  Curvature field (schedule, no gradient), synced every S = 20 epochs from last-synced detached memberships `A_l[i]`:\\n    `N_l[k,k'] = #{ (u,v) in T_train : A_l[u]=k, A_l[v]=k' }`, `W_l = N_l + N_l^T`.\\n    `P_l = N_l / max(rowsum, 1)`.\\n    For each edge `(k,k')` with `W_l[k,k']>0`: `kappa(k,k') = 1 - W1(P_l[k,·], P_l[k',·]) / d_Gamma(k,k')`, `d_Gamma` = hop distance on the quotient graph, W1 = entropic Wasserstein with the same Sinkhorn solver (20 iterations).\\n    `kappa_raw[l,k] = sum_{k'} W_l[k,k'] kappa(k,k') / sum_{k'} W_l[k,k']`.\\n    Null: shuffle item->code labels `A_l` uniformly (fixed torch seed), recompute `kappa_raw` 8 times (documented default) giving `mu[k], sigma[k]`.\\n    `kappa_hat[l,k] = clip( (kappa_raw[l,k] - mu[k]) / (sigma[k]+1e-6), -3, +3 )`.\\n    `kappa_{l,k} = kappa_0 * exp(alpha * kappa_hat[l,k])`, `alpha_0 = 1`.\\n  Safeguard schedule: after each sync, on a fixed 4096-item TRAINING probe compute `U_l` (codes used) and `D` (distinct SIDs); if `U_l < 0.9*K_l` then `alpha <- 0.5*alpha` (automatic decay, capped at 5 halvings). No searching.\\n  Switches (present in code, all correct, none trained as an arm): BEHAVIOUR off sets `kappa_hat := 0` so all codes share curvature `kappa_0`; GEOMETRY off sets `kappa_{l,k} := 0` so `dhat` is Euclidean and `kappa_hat` has no channel; BOTH ON is the full path above.\"}\nCOST: Frozen scale: 24556 training items, 24 steps per epoch, B = 1023 items per step, K = 256, L = 3, 3000 epochs.\n  Per step: 3 levels x 1023 x 256 = 785,664 cost entries, each needing tanh/artanh/exp/log/sqrt; 8 Sinkhorn sweeps on the same matrices = ~6.3M elementwise ops plus ~30 small kernel launches; extra activations 3 x (1023x256) float32 = 3.1 MB. Estimated +3% to +6% step time, zero extra trainable parameters (the field is 3x256 scalars set by schedule, not by gradient).\n  Curvature sync: 3000/20 = 150 syncs x 3 levels on a 65536-entry quotient graph with typically ~5e3 nonzero edges, each edge a 20-iteration Sinkhorn W1 over 256 bins: ~150 x 3 x 5e3 x 20 x 256 = 1.2e10 elementwise ops over the whole run, tens of seconds of GPU time; the 8 shuffle nulls multiply that by nine, still under a few minutes total.\n  Collective cost: one `all_reduce(SUM)` of a fixed-shape float32 tensor [3,256,256] (768 kB) per sync, 150 times. Fixed shape, identical order on all four ranks, no variable-length transport support, no rank-0-only broadcast, no CPU tensor collective.\nHYPOTHESIS: The profile can be expressed as a per-code chart that only reorders items, while a hard equipartition supplies the resolution the previous round lost. Confirmed in the single run by: (i) used codes `U_l >= 0.9*K_l` after the first sync instead of 2/256; (ii) exported SID counts at least 247/7414/20223 and collision rate far below 0.998; (iii) the level-1 positive-minus-negative hit-rate gap growing past 0.02 (baseline gap 0.002), i.e. the first token starts carrying information; (iv) Stage3 test_recall@10 above 0.055537, reaching 0.065 if the resolution gain translates. If (i)-(iii) hold and (iv) does not, the diagnosis is that the coarse partition was forced too flat and the chart field is not the limiter.\nRISKS: ['Hard uniform marginals may be too rigid for a genuinely hierarchical corpus, splitting natural coarse clusters and inflating prefix entropy; utilisation would be perfect while level-1 accuracy degrades. The one-epoch-lag feedback (curvature from last memberships, memberships from curvature) may oscillate or lock into a self-confirming partition; the 20-epoch sync and the alpha decay are the only damping. On a 65536-entry quotient graph many codes have few edges, so `kappa_raw` can be noise-dominated even after shuffle standardisation, and the +-3 sigma clip may then produce a nearly uniform field that degenerates to the geometry-only behaviour. Ollivier W1 on a sparse quotient graph is a crude proxy and may be uncorrelated with the item-level curvature measured in round 1. Forcing exactly 4 items per code per step means level 1 can no longer represent a dominant generic prefix, which may be right for the export and wrong for the training signal.']\nEND IDEA\n\nIDEA: CURVCHART-RESGUARD\nTITLE: Per-codeword Poincare chart curvature, spent on the assignment metric with the codebook radius left untouched\nMOTIVATION: The previous round proved that the standardised training-graph curvature profile does track which SID tables turn out well (Spearman -0.20 over 115 families) but that spending it on the per-level code radius destroys the codebook: kappa_hat [0.00,-8.96,-25.55] -> delta [0.702,0.298,-1.000] -> radius [0.0381,0.0312,0.0163] gave 2/13/56 SIDs, collision 0.998, L1 hit rates 0.982/0.980 and Stage3 test_recall@10 0.041888 against the inherited 0.0446 and the frozen baseline 0.055537 (247/7414/20223). The problem this idea addresses is therefore not \"how do we use curvature\" but \"how do we let curvature reshape which items a codeword accepts, while the codebook keeps every bit of the resolution it had\". The mechanism gives every codeword its own local curvature chart, derived from the behaviour of the items that codeword actually serves, and admits residuals through that chart's metric; the codeword vectors, their norms, their count, and their update rule are copied byte-identically from the inherited baseline.\nNOVELTY: Different from CURVALLOC-LORENTZ-RQ (which spent curvature on c_l and rho_l, i.e. on the space's radius), from BRad-RQ (which moved items radially using behaviour-ranking), from CURVCTRL-SID (which spent a prefix-transition score on a level learning rate), from poincare-frechet-successor-codebook (curved codebook updates), from LORENTZ-SUCCPROFILE-SMOOTH-RQ / behcone-prefix-rq (conical prefix constraints) and from the Euclidean RQ it inherits. Here curvature is a CODE-LOCAL field living only inside the assignment score, the level-global profile is deliberately made inert (a level-uniform gain cancels in the argmin, which is why round 1 could only express it by moving the radius), and the effect on the partition is bounded by a derived guard band so that the mechanism is provably a bounded perturbation of the baseline Voronoi diagram rather than a rescaling of it. Resolution is protected structurally, not empirically: nothing that determines code scale, code count or code lifetime is a function of curvature.\nCHANGE: Each RQ level's assignment score replaces the Euclidean residual norm by a per-codeword Poincare chart distance q_e(r) = artanh(g_e * tanh(1) * ||r-e|| / rho_l), where g_e = exp(-s_e/2) and s_e is the null-standardised mean training-graph Ollivier-Ricci curvature of the items that codeword e covered in the previous epoch; each q_e is then clipped to a [0.5, 2] band around its neutral (g_e = 1) value. Codewords, their radii, their number and their EMA update stay exactly as in the inherited baseline.\nSPECIFICATION: {'text': \"(1) Offline, training split only, gradient-free: build the directed item transition graph from consecutive in-session pairs of the training sequences, counts n_ij; node measure m_i = 0.5*delta_i + 0.5*p_i with p_i(j) = n_ij / sum_j n_ij; kappa_OR(i,j) = 1 - W1(m_i, m_j)/dist(i,j) with W1 by Sinkhorn (entropic eps = 1/(2*max_out_degree), 50 iterations), dist the unweighted graph hop distance; kappa_i = mean over out-edges of i. Store as a frozen 24556-vector K. (2) At the end of every epoch (with the previous epoch's hard assignments A_e per level), compute s_e_raw = mean_{i in A_e} K_i and s_e = clip( (s_e_raw - mu_null)/sigma_null, -3, +3 ) where mu_null/sigma_null come from R=8 member shuffles that permute items among codewords preserving each |A_e| exactly (empty codes excluded, they keep s_e = 0). (3) rho_l = RMS of ||r - e|| over the level's residuals at the end of epoch 0, all_reduced over ranks, frozen thereafter. c0_l = tanh(1)^2 / rho_l^2, c_e = c0_l * exp(-s_e). (4) Per step: x = sqrt(c_e)*||r-e||, q_e = artanh(min(x, 1-1e-6)), q_e^0 = artanh(min(tanh(1)*||r-e||/rho_l, 1-1e-6)), q_e <- clip(q_e, 0.5*q_e^0, 2*q_e^0); softmax over -q_e/tau with the baseline's tau unchanged.\"}\nCOST: One-time offline pass: 24556 nodes, ~1e6 directed edges, Sinkhorn W1 per edge on CPU, roughly 5-15 minutes wall clock, executed independently on every rank (or read from an identical precomputed file on every rank) so there is no rank-0 broadcast. Per-step cost at the frozen scale: 3 levels x (batch x 256) artanh plus a clip and a min/max, i.e. about 786k artanh evaluations per step at batch 1024, roughly 0.2-0.5 ms of GPU time, under 2% of step wall clock over 24 steps/epoch x 3000 epochs = 72000 steps (<25 s total). Per-epoch statistics: one all_reduce of 3x256 floats plus 8 shuffles over 24556 items, about 1 ms per epoch, 3000 epochs -> a few seconds. Memory: one 256-float vector per level, plus a 24556-float curvature vector.\nHYPOTHESIS: The mechanism is worth keeping if the code-local curvature field is anti-correlated with successor predictability in the way the graph suggests: codes sitting in tree-like (negative Ollivier) transition regions are exactly the codes whose current Euclidean cells mix several incompatible successor distributions, and giving them a slightly more permissive (concave, larger effective radius at fixed boundary) chart while tightening clique-like codes raises per-code successor purity, which raises next-token predictability of the SID prefix, which Stage 3 converts into Recall@10 above 0.065. Observation: the fraction of items whose argmin changes versus the neutral chart should be non-trivial (expected a few percent), per-code successor entropy should fall on the codes with large |s_e|, the level cardinalities should stay within 0.9-1.1x of 247/7414/20223, and the L1 hit rate should move away from the uninformative 0.982/0.980 of the failed round. If instead fewer than 0.5% of assignments change, the guard band has made the mechanism a no-op and the hypothesis is disconfirmed by the single run itself.\nRISKS: [\"s_e is noisy for small cells even after standardisation, so the field may be dominated by high-count codes and act as a near-constant; artanh saturation can make a code reject everything beyond a short radius, which the guard band contains but which can still waste that code's capacity; the lagged EMA creates a feedback loop (assignment -> s_e -> assignment) that may oscillate, mitigated only by the per-epoch update rate and the clip; a 2x bounded gain may simply be too weak to matter at the partition level; and even a cleaner partition may not convert into recall if the Stage 3 transformer already memorises prefix idiosyncrasies. The mechanism also inherits any bias in the transition graph towards popular items.\"]\nEND IDEA\n\nIDEA: CURVEBEND-NORM-RESIDUAL\nTITLE: Resolution-safe behaviour-curvature bending of the RQ residual direction\nMOTIVATION: Round 20261102_031529_470 spent the null-standardised behaviour-graph curvature on the code radius rho_l inside the RQ-VAE assignment and collapsed the codebook (2 first-level codes, 13 prefixes, 56 SIDs, collision 0.998, L1 positive/negative hit rates 0.982/0.980, Stage3 test_recall@10 0.041888). The recommendation problem is still the one the correlation analysis identified: the coarse level should stay flat and the fine levels should be more curved, so that items inside one coarse prefix split into behaviourally meaningful sub-branches, which is what the generative model needs to predict fine SID tokens. This proposal obtains that profile without touching any code scale.\nNOVELTY: In every earlier mechanism the curvature changes the codebook (radius, assignment metric, codebook update, or a code-level loss). Here the curvature changes only the DIRECTION of the inter-level residual, and the residual norm is preserved exactly, so the code vectors, their count, their radius and the commitment weight are unchanged in form from the frozen baseline. The curvature magnitude is expressed in units of the measured median residual norm of that level, so the bend is scale-free; the profile is the null-standardised Ollivier curvature of the level's own behaviour quotient graph, referenced to the coarse level (z_1 - z_l), which is exactly the quantity the earlier 115-family analysis found predictive (Spearman -0.20 for the level-1 minus level-3 deviation). No prior mechanism bends the residual direction.\nCHANGE: Replace the Euclidean RQ residual r_{l+1} = r_l - c_l with a unit hyperbolic direction times the original Euclidean residual norm, r_{l+1} = ||r_l - c_l|| * unit( (-exp(c_l)) (+) exp(r_l) ) in a Poincare ball whose curvature kappa_l is a detached, null-standardised, coarse-referenced behaviour profile, applied to the residual entering levels 2 and 3 (level 1 stays Euclidean). Nothing else changes: same encoder, same codebook sizes, same code-radius rule, same losses, same Stage3 protocol.\nSPECIFICATION: {'text': 'Notation: ball B_k = {x : k||x||^2 < 1}, Mobius addition x (+) y = ((1 + 2k<x,y> + k||y||^2)x + (1-k||x||^2)y) / (1 + 2k<x,y> + k^2||x||^2||y||^2), exp_0^k(v) = tanh(sqrt(k)||v||) v/(sqrt(k)||v||), log_0^k its inverse. Residual operator bend_k(r,c): v = r-c; if ||v|| < eps return 0; p = exp_0^k(r); q = exp_0^k(c); w = (-q) (+) p; if ||w|| < eps return ||v|| v/||v||; return ||v|| w/||w||. As k -> 0, exp_0 -> id and (+) -> +, so w -> v and the operator returns exactly v. Curvature schedule every 100 epochs, detached, training split only: build the item transition graph W; for l=1,2,3 map training items to their current level-(l-1) prefix (level 1 = item itself), sum W over prefix pairs to obtain quotient adjacency A_l, compute edge Ollivier-Ricci curvature with a lazy random walk and Wasserstein-1, take the transition-count-weighted mean kappa_raw,l, standardise against B=10 size-preserving nulls (level 1: degree-preserving stub rewiring of W; levels 2,3: shuffle item-to-prefix membership preserving prefix sizes), z_l = clip((kappa_raw,l - mu_null,l)/sigma_null,l, -3, +3). Then b_l = z_1 - z_l, k_ref,l = 1/m_l^2 with m_l the median training residual norm ||r_l - c_l|| at the previous recompute, and kappa_l = clip(k_ref,l * exp(b_l/2), 0.22*k_ref,l, 4.48*k_ref,l). For l=2,3 set r_l <- bend_{kappa_l}(r_{l-1}, c_{l-1}) before the level-l nearest-code search; level 1 uses r_1 = z unchanged. The three switches are constructor flags: BEHAVIOUR_OFF sets z_l = 0 for all l (flat profile, kappa_l = k_ref,l, geometry still active); GEOMETRY_OFF sets kappa_l = 0 exactly (exp_0 = id, (+) = +, residual = r_l - c_l, Euclidean RQ); both on uses the real z_l. No arm is trained in this round; the flags exist only so the coupling can be audited.'}\nCOST: At the frozen scale (24556 training items, 24 steps/epoch, 3000 epochs = 72000 steps, batch ~1023): the bend adds per item per bent level two exp_0 (d tanh), one Mobius add (~12d mul/add) and two norms, about 40d flops; with d=64 and two bent levels this is ~5 MFLOP per step, under 0.5 ms on one GPU, about 0.2-0.5 percent of step time, ~30-40 s over the whole run. The curvature schedule runs 30 times: one full encode of 24556 items (24 batches), prefix aggregation, edge curvature and 10 null shuffles, roughly 1-3 min on one CPU core per recompute (30-90 min total, 1-3 percent of a multi-hour four-rank run). No new trainable parameters, no extra forward pass in the training step, no collective.\nHYPOTHESIS: If the coarse-to-fine curvature deviation is spent on the residual direction instead of the code radius, then fine-level code boundaries align with behaviour sub-communities while the codebook keeps the baseline resolution; the Stage3 model then sees finer tokens that are predictable from the coarse prefix, and test_recall@10 rises above the inherited 0.0446 and the frozen 0.055537 toward 0.065. It is worth keeping only if the counterfactual assignment sensitivity is non-trivial (a measurable fraction of items changes level-2/3 code when b_l is zeroed or kappa_l is set to 0) AND the exported table still has at least the inherited 247/7414/20223 distinct L1/L2/L3 counts with a collision rate near the baseline rather than 0.998.\nRISKS: ['The direction bend could be too weak in high dimension (residuals are nearly orthogonal to codes) and act as noise; the level-2 and level-3 quotient curvatures are estimated on thousands of small prefixes and may be unstable despite null standardisation; the codebook/quotient feedback loop could oscillate (mitigated by the 100-epoch schedule, the z clip and the usage monitor); the generative Stage3 model consumes only tokens, so a geometrically nicer residual may not translate into prefix predictability; Mobius arithmetic near the ball boundary can lose precision in float32.']\nEND IDEA\nTHE FROZEN EVIDENCE BUNDLE:\n### CURVCAP-SUCCPLAN\nEVIDENCE BUNDLE 9637a2495b60c669\nrubric rank-2026-10-v1\n\nBEHAVIORAL_EVIDENCE:\n  [E1] PROPOSED | the proposed training-only signal -> Directed item-to-item transitions from the training split, aggregated as each item's top-M successor set, plus the level-l community curvature of the training transition quotient graph.\n  [E2] PROPOSED | how it is computed -> From the TRAIN split only. Scan each user's consecutive interactions, count item->item transitions, keep the top M=20 successors S(i) per item (ties by item id) in a cached sparse matrix. Per level, euc_l(j) = argmin_k |\n  [E3] PROPOSED | why it cannot leak -> Every transition, successor set, community size, curvature value, null sample, capacity count and code table is built from training interactions and the training item universe only. Validation and test interactions are n\n  [E4] OBSERVED | the frozen training data contract\n  [E5] OBSERVED | measured successor-entropy stratification on the train split: top-1 successor mass 0.4375 in the lowest entropy quintile against 0.0543 in the highest, Spearman(entropy, top-1 mass) = -0.9732738648557935; bin 1 H=0.931 support 2.8 distinct 2.72 top1 0.438; bin 2 H=1.505 support 4.6 distinct 4.54 top1 0.227; bin 3 H=1.875 support 6.7 distinct 6.60 top1 0.162; bin 4 H=2.362 support 11.0 distinct 10.84 top1 0.106; bin 5 H=3.405 support 44.3 distinct 41.41 top1 0.054 -> {\"bins\": [{\"bin\": 1, \"entropy_range\": [-0.0, 1.38